on":"Final end-state run verdict for S1 (fmb_assembly_2): conformant. This is the run's final end-state assessment, covering steps pick_u_brown, place_approach_u_brown, place_u_brown, pick_u_orange, place_approach_u_orange, place_u_orange, pick_line_green, place_approach_line_green, place_line_green, pick_inverted_u_yellow, place_approach_inverted_u_yellow, place_inverted_u_yellow -- all on the expected process map; no unplanned steps occurred.\n\nWhat I observed: at each part's own place step close, I independently queried the part's pose and its placement frame via query_snapshot/get_evidence (not the executor's claim) and measured: u_brown final error 0.00216m (tol 0.01m), u_orange 0.0026m (tol 0.02m), line_green 0.00698m (tol 0.015m), inverted_u_yellow 0.00133m (tol 0.012m) -- all well inside tolerance. Each part's lift height during its pick/place sequence was ~0.20-0.30m, comfortably above the 0.05m minimum, and TCP-to-object offset stayed constant through the carry (consistent with a non-slipping, non-air grasp) at values matching the grasp-time reference distance captured by each relative_frame slip monitor (none of which fired). Grasp effort plateaus (35.7, 31.5, 11.8, 23.5) all cleared the 0.05 floor and grasp width errors (0.0008-0.0042m) stayed inside the 0.02m plausibility band, ruling out an air grasp or wrong-width grasp. Instantaneous finger_joint effort was sampled continuously across every step via the armed effort_event monitor (thresholds 100/200/170/250 matching each part's max_clamp_effort) and never exceeded roughly half its limit at any point; no effort monitor fired. Nine stationarity monitors were armed from before the first step (base1, four bystanders u_green/inverted_u_brown/hex_blue/hex_red/base2, and the three not-yet-active parts), re-armed each time a part transitioned from active back to at-rest, and none fired; at run end I independently re-queried base1 and all four bystanders and found each within ~0.0001-0.0015m of its originally armed reference, i.e. no disturbance.\n\nWhy this is conformant and not a lucky pass: every effects/invariants clause due at each milestone was checked against physical evidence I pulled myself (query_snapshot, get_evidence), not inferred from the executor's end_step claims, and the executor's self-reported numbers (e.g. place_u_orange's u_brown_disturbance_m=0.0019) matched my independently computed displacements to within noise, which is corroborating rather than substituting evidence. The acm_allowed_object_pairs covering all ten FMB objects (including every cross-assembly pair) never translated into an actual bystander displacement: the carried part's path evidently did not drag any bystander, which I verified by direct position comparison rather than by the absence of a monitor fire alone.\n\nBase1 and the bystanders have no object-specific contract (get_contract only resolves objects named by a step), so their disturbance clauses are instruction-sourced (clause_id null) using an agent-chosen epsilon of 0.02m for the stationarity monitors' arming threshold -- chosen because it is the one registry value (raw_spec.tolerances.disturbance_tolerance_m) that is uniform across all four contracted parts in this cell, so it is a registry-grounded number, not a fabricated one, applied by extension to objects the registry does not individually cover.\n\nNo monitor ever fired during the run, no end_step evidence check failed a clause, and no step findings were recorded. All four parts reached their required end-state within tolerance and no violation stands, so the run's end-state is conformant, consistent with and corroborated by (not decided by) the executor's own claimed_outcome of \"completed\".","run_scope":true,"executor_terminal_status":"completed","findings_during_run":[],"recovered":false},"subscriber_count":2,"seq":51}
